export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  "108|chokhmat-shlomo|11|_": "Section 11 — One who erred in Minchah, etc. Marginal note: Behold, in this there are views that one need not return and pray at Motzei Shabbos — what does he gain by praying more, etc.; and see what I wrote — clear proof for this in my novellae on the Gemara Pesachim 95b from what is written there: their reason is because of impurity of the threshold — he should return and do it in impurity, even though then there were only a minority impure and the majority performing were pure; and here the majority performing Pesach were impure and impurity was permitted or deferred in public — from this it is proven we do not follow the present but what was then at the time of the primary obligation, not at the time of make-up prayers; and as they say, the two make-up prayers of the first are for Motzei Shabbos; understand well.**** END BLOCK ****",
  "108|chokhmat-shlomo|12|_": "Section 12 — One who errs and mentions an occurrence from other days, etc. NB: I was asked during Chanukah about someone who said \"on the miracles\" in the blessing of Avodah before \"ve-Tachazinah,\" and he had already shifted his feet. I answered that he must go back, for it is obvious both for the Shulchan Aruch and for the Taz that they do not disagree except regarding whether at least the substance of the mention is fitting in that place — then it would be akin to the blessing itself and it would not be an interruption in the body of the prayer formula, but rather it is not its time today and the delay is from another side; in this the Shulchan Aruch holds it is not an interruption and the Taz disagrees. But if he mentions something that is not fitting in that blessing, even if it is its time today, nevertheless what of it — since it is not fitting in that blessing it is an interruption in the body of the prayer formula. And some say if he already shifted his feet, in such a case for him it would be mere conversation and is an interruption, as the Taz wrote here and above (siman 104) that wherever one shifted his feet he must go back. Also if he did not shift his feet, it seems he should go back, for on the contrary if it were not its time today at all it would only be mere conversation and that would suffice to go back as he wishes. But here, since it is its time today and he mentioned it not in its place, it is worse, per what we say in tractate Zevachim (2a) that a suitable type destroys it but an unsuitable type does not destroy it — so too here, since it is its time today, to remember it not in its place one must go back to the beginning in every respect. However, since we have no clear proof, he should have intent and pray as a voluntary prayer out of doubt. But if he shifted his feet he certainly goes back, even if it happened so in Maariv prayer, as with any other complete error that one goes back even for Maariv, and as if he did not mention Yaaleh ve-Yavo on Yom Tov with Choshen Mishpat — and so it is correct, examine well. See in the Taz what he argues on the Mechaber's ruling, and see in my novellae on the laws of Chanukah siman 577 in my work Orach Chayyim from the year 5664 — a great proof for the Taz's view from the Gemara, and what I explained there why to reject Rabbeinu Yonah's proof, etc., with Hashem's help examine well.",
  "108|chokhmat-shlomo|8|_": "Section 8 — One who did not pray, etc. Marginal note: Behold, from this it is proven that forgetting is coercion; see in Magen Avraham what he discussed on this. And to me it seems to prove that forgetting is coercion: behold, initially it was difficult to me in what is written in the Gemara in chapter 4 of Bava Metzia (51b) — there it says Shmuel said: I say even according to R' Meir, etc. — R' Meir did not say there he certainly uproots; but here who will say he uproots? And it was very difficult for me how to interpret an explicit mishnah at the end of chapter 6 of Peah, thus: one who says 'I will harvest on condition that what I forget I may take' — he has [the right of] forgetting; and Rambam, Rosh, and Ra'avad explained from the power that any condition on what is written in the Torah — his condition is void. And it is difficult: behold there it is doubtful uprooting, for perhaps in any case he will not forget, and it is literally like 'on condition you have no claim of fraud against me.' And behold this would settle: one could say since we see R' Yehuda holds in monetary matters his condition stands — if so we see monetary law is lighter than prohibition; if so R' Meir holds even in monetary matters his condition is void — namely only where he certainly uproots; but in doubtful uprooting R' Meir concedes in monetary matters his condition stands. But in a matter that is not monetary, even in doubtful uprooting it is a condition on what is written in the Torah. But still, what shall we do with the topic in Gittin (64b) regarding 'on condition you eat meat' — there too it challenges that it was a condition on what is written in the Torah, and Ravina answers there too: it must be like other clothing and conjugal rights where he certainly uproots; but here 'you shall not eat' and 'you shall not divorce' — and so it was copied in Shulchan Aruch Avodah Zarah (simanim 38 and 144) — if so it is explicit that even regarding prohibition we say where it is not certain uprooting it is a condition on what is written in the Torah. And it is difficult: behold an explicit mishnah in Peah that this too is a condition on what is written in the Torah even though it is not certain uprooting. And at first glance this requires investigation; it must be explained that there it was in her power not to eat and not to divorce, and therefore anything in her power is not considered a condition on what is written in the Torah; but here it is not in his power not to forget, therefore it is a condition on what is written in the Torah. If so it works out if we say forgetting is literally coercion — then it is fine to say here, since it is not in his power, it was a condition on what is written in the Torah. But if we say forgetting is negligence — it must be proven that it was in his power to be careful not to forget; if so it was also in his power, and it is difficult why it would be a condition on what is written in the Torah when it is not certain uprooting. And certainly the matter depends: if it is literal coercion it was not in his power to guard against it; and if he was negligent it must be in his power to guard — for if it were not in his power to guard, that is not negligence. If so, if we say it was in his power not to forget, why was it considered there a condition on what is written in the Torah? It must be proven forgetting is not in his power, and it is proven it is literal coercion. And this is clear proof to me that forgetting is coercion; understand well.**** END BLOCK ****",
  "110|chokhmat-shlomo|7|_": "Seif 7 — he says it, etc. N.B. behold regarding the law of tefillat ha-derekh, the view of Magen Avraham 14 is that we require he has established himself on the road outside the border of the city — see what I wrote on this in my composition Turei Zahav on Yoreh De'ah 47 in the laws of mixtures siman 101, and in my composition siman 333; see what I wrote there in passing, with Hashem's help, and examine closely.",
  "110|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "<small>Section 4.</small> He shall pray sitting with one leg folded. And the reason he does not open with \"Baruch\" — since the blessing \"Who hears prayer\" is in its place in the prayer, it is adjacent — Rabbeinu Yonah at the beginning of Berachos.",
  "110|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6 — in order to attach it. See Tur siman 6 and Bach there.",
  "111|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3 — that one juxtaposes redemption — in my humble opinion on Shabbat and the like he prays with the congregation and afterwards recites the Recitation of Shema, since on Shabbat there is no obligation at all to juxtapose redemption to prayer. And see siman 236 seif 3.",
  "113|chokhmat-shlomo|9|_": "Seif 9: One should not add, etc. NB: Behold I saw in responsa Gur Aryeh Yehudah, Orach Chayyim part (siman 105), that he brought in the name of the Peri Megadim in a responsum that he raised difficulty: why in \"Elokei Avraham, Elokei Yitzchak\" is vav not stated, but in Yaakov it says \"ve-Elokei Yaakov\" — see there what he wrote on this. And behold the Peri Megadim's answers are not to be examined; and in truth the verse is full in parashat Va'era: \"to Avraham, to Yitzchak, and to Yaakov,\" and vav is stated with Yaakov and not with Yitzchak — see in Zohar there what he wrote, because of a hint that Yaakov is the essence (see there). And from this grew the language of prayer clearly to distinguish Yaakov with vav; see also what I wrote on the reason of the matter in my chiddushim already, and in my chiddushei Torah of the year 552 from parashat Vayeitzei — what more he wrote on another reason per the words of the Zohar there, and the words of the Zohar in parashat Va'era (see there); also in Zohar Vayikra on the verse \"and a soul that sins\" there is another reason why vav is not stated — because we need to include Yitzchak in Avraham (see there) — examine well. And now it further appears: behold Hashem, called Yaakov the firstborn, as written in the midrash in parashat Bo, in parashat Kadesh Li Kol Bekhor, and in prayer \"congregation of Yaakov, Your firstborn son\" — the intention in this is: behold in the redemption from Egypt it is stated first Avraham, etc.; but in the future redemption it says \"and I will remember My covenant with Yaakov,\" etc. — behold Yaakov is stated first; if so, Hashem, made him firstborn to the Patriarchs, and this will not be fulfilled until the future to come; then Yaakov will become firstborn to the Patriarchs. Until now Avraham was the head. And behold it is known that vav adds to the first matter; therefore at first they begin to say \"Elokei Avraham, Elokei Yitzchak\" as it is now in this time, and afterward they say \"ve-Elokei Yaakov,\" who is destined to be first before Avraham; therefore vav is stated, to interrupt and say that in him is a matter on its own, and in the future they will begin with him first in the future to come to be the firstborn to the Patriarchs — so it appears; examine well. And it further appears to say per what is written in tractate Bava Metzia p. 87, that Eliyahu said it is impossible for all of them to stand together, for they would increase mercy and the Messiah would come before his time (see there). If so, likewise in saying \"Elokei Avraham\" Avraham is aroused above, and likewise Yitzchak, and likewise Yaakov; and if one mentioned all of them in combination, all would be aroused together above and would bring the Messiah before his time. Therefore one mentions Avraham by himself and Yitzchak by himself; and in Yaakov — even if Yitzchak and Yaakov stand together — they will not bring the Messiah before his time, since each is one relative to the other: Yitzchak is judgment and Yaakov is mercy. But if Avraham were also there, there would be two mercies and one judgment, and they would overpower the judgment; for even though this one is on this side and that one on that side, with Yitzchak in the middle they would sweeten the judgment and bring the Messiah before his time. Therefore they mention Yitzchak without vav — and it fits well; examine well. And see in my composition Chokhmat Shlomo on the Torah, with Hashem's help, parashat Shemot, year 554, what he wrote per the above, with Hashem's help — examine well.",
  "114|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "(Seif 6 in the gloss) \"Three blessings,\" etc. NB — see in Magen Avraham sk 9, what he wrote that however, if one erred in the middle, one does not bless at all. And behold in siman 119, sk 5, he wrote: if one erred or skipped — meaning if one skipped the blessing entirely, or erred in not saying it as the Sages enacted, etc. And behold it would seem the matters contradict, but they do not contradict: for in siman 119 the intention is that one did not say something of the opening near the closing, or one closed or opened improperly; but if one opened and closed and mentioned something of the opening near the closing, even though one skipped in the middle, one need not return; and similarly Magen Avraham noted to see siman 59, where the intention is explained — that even if one said in the middle \"He Who brings on evenings,\" one has fulfilled [the obligation]; and this is not like Ohr Zarua, who wrote; and it requires study there; examine well:",
  "114|chokhmat-shlomo|9|_": "(Seif 9) If on the first day of Pesach one says the blessing \"You are mighty\" until \"Who makes the dew descend\" ninety times, etc. NB — the Tur wrote, and this is its language: and Maharam of Rothenburg was accustomed in Shulchan Aruch to say the blessing \"You are mighty,\" etc., and his proof from the chapter \"How is the habit\" — for we say regarding an ox that is prone [to gore]: \"If he distanced its goring it is liable; if he brought its goring near — is it not all the more so?\" etc. And Rashba wrote: I have not seen the elderly rabbis of France do so, for the case is not similar to the proof — for there the reason is because it was established to gore; and if it was established with three distant [gorings], all the more so with three near ones; but rain, which was fixed in prayer and the matter depends on habit of speech — we do not say thus, etc. And behold at first glance it seemed to me that Maharam of Rothenburg's intention was for what is said there, in the Gemara: \"It was asked — for designation of an ox or for designation of a man? A difference — if three groups of witnesses came in one day: if you say for designation of an ox, it is designated; if you say for designation of a man, it is not designated.\" They said: \"Now he is testifying about him — what is the law?\" And it is difficult for me: how could one think it is for designation of a man? How does R. Meir disagree and say a fortiori: \"If he distanced its goring it is liable; if he brought its goring near — is it not all the more so?\" It works out well if it is for designation of an ox — then the a fortiori is fine; but if it is for designation of a man, this is not an a fortiori — for in three days, with this, remembrance is fixed in his heart that it is established, and he has a guardian; but if he designated on one day, one could say he forgot the matter and it left his heart, and therefore he did not guard it; and necessarily it is proven that even regarding a matter that depends on memory of the heart, one remembers more in near time than in distant time. And from this it is proven that the same applies to \"Who makes the wind blow.\" However, as we analyzed, one may uphold the words of Rashba — and that he made the Gemara difficult: why does it say a difference if three groups came in one day, and not also say the reverse — if three groups came in three days, but all testified about three gorings of one day — for example, that it gored them all in one day — then if it is for designation of an ox it does not help, and if for designation of a man it helps. And behold from this it is proven that there is no doubt at all if it is for designation of a creature alone — it is simply clear to him that we require the gorings to be on three days; only it was asked whether we require designation of a man as well, or designation of an ox alone suffices; and so is the intention of the Gemara — whether for designation of an ox alone, or we require designation of a man as well. If so, the words of R. Meir would be thus: the reason of R. Yehudah, who holds that for designation of a man we require three days, is since he holds that for designation of an ox we require three days — if so, as the judgment of the ox, so the judgment of the owners; and its end judgment is like its beginning judgment; and since its beginning judgment requires three days, its end judgment also requires three days; but if its beginning judgment did not require three days, all the more so at its end for designation of owners we do not require three days. And accordingly the words of R. Meir do not come to him — that it deals with designation of an ox, and he is not uncertain — only in the words of R. Yehudah, whether it deals with designation of an ox alone or designation of a man as well; and if so, we have no proof for the words of Maharam. But accordingly we would have proof to the contrary for the words of Maharam — that he made the Gemara itself difficult: why is it not also asked regarding the reverse? Perhaps one could say that R. Yehudah holds that for designation of a man alone we require three days, but for designation of an ox we do not care at all; and why is the reverse not asked of him as well? And necessarily he cannot ask thus — for then the a fortiori of R. Meir would go away: \"If he distanced its goring it is liable; if he brought its goring near — is it not all the more so?\" — for regarding designation of a man, which depends on memory, this is not an a fortiori; for a matter done day after day over much time is fixed more in one's heart than what is done much in a short time; and necessarily the a fortiori of R. Meir applies only to designation of an ox; and so for R. Yehudah, designation of an ox certainly requires [three days], and there is no doubt — only whether designation of a man is also required; and from this it is proven like the view of Rashba and not like the view of Maharam of Rothenburg — for according to Maharam it is difficult why he is not also uncertain regarding the reverse, whether designation of an ox alone also suffices, as above; examine well. It also seems, in my humble opinion, to resolve the difficulty of the Acharonim on Maharam of Rothenburg mentioned above — that he brought proof from what R. Meir said: \"If he distanced its goring it is liable; if he brought its goring near — is it not all the more so?\" — and they wondered at this, for we do not rule like R. Meir but like R. Yehudah; see there. And it seems to me thus: there is [room] to say that the reason for the dispute of R. Meir and R. Yehudah is thus — for at first glance the words of R. Meir are clear: if distance is a presumption, all the more so nearness. But it appears they disagree in the dispute of the poskim whether forgetting is coercion or negligence. And accordingly, behold in chapter 3 of Ketubot we say that regarding plotting witnesses one does not find warning — for if they were warned from the outset they would say \"I forgot\"; if so here too, since the obligation of the goring ox is as explicit in the verse \"and it was testified to its owner and he did not guard it\" — he can say \"it was that it was testified about him\" in any case he forgot afterward that it became established, and therefore he did not guard it; but this works out only if forgetting is coercion; but if forgetting is negligence, then on this very matter he is liable for what he was negligent in. And in plotting witnesses, even if they were negligent, one does not obligate them lashes for negligence — only if they testified with actual intent and not for negligence; but monetary payments one is liable for even negligence. Therefore one could say that here they disagree: certainly regarding establishing the body of the ox to gore, the a fortiori of R. Meir is correct — if he distanced its goring he is liable, all the more so if he brought its goring near. But regarding the owners, who could not say \"I forgot\" — it is the reverse: if it was testified about him for three days in order, he is not apt to forget; but if all were testified on one day, he is apt to forget and can say \"I forgot.\" Therefore R. Meir holds that forgetting is negligence; therefore the main matter depends on whether the body of the ox was established to gore — and with this the a fortiori is well learned: if he distanced its goring he is liable, if he brought its goring near — is it not all the more so? But R. Yehudah, who holds that forgetting is coercion — if so, even though the ox became a goring ox, nevertheless the owners can say: since it is written \"and it was testified to its owner and he did not guard it,\" they can say \"I forgot\"; therefore we require that it be over three days, and then with the multiplicity of days it is certainly negligence if he forgot; and with this, for all opinions forgetting is negligence; and therefore over three days it is negligence if he forgot, but on one day it is not negligence if he forgot, and he can say \"I forgot.\" And even if they testified to me over three days — since the goring was on one day, and for testimony we require that one be fit to testify immediately — but if one is not fit to testify immediately, it does not help if one testifies afterward — for its beginning was in disqualification and its end in fitness; similarly here, if it gored them all on one day — if so, if they all testify today it is not considered established; and since they are not fit to testify today, it does not help if they testify afterward over several days. And therefore it comes out well that there, regarding establishment, it depends on the owners and one can say \"I forgot\"; therefore here three days are preferable to one day. But to establish oneself to say \"Who makes the wind blow\" — he himself was established to be presumed to say as he was accustomed, and this does not depend on his thought but only on what is accustomed in his mouth — as we hold in a doubt regarding Shema: if one began \"so that your days may be multiplied,\" then one certainly finishes; and it would be if hearing that one holds the chain and goes — if so we see that we rely on what one is accustomed to in this; and certainly one learns an a fortiori from the ox, which itself is certainly an a fortiori: if with distance it is so, all the more so with nearness. And the words of Maharam of Rothenburg come out well, with Hashem's help; and even if Maharam did not intend this, in any case it is already written in the early authorities in the abridged [commentary] on Ta'anit, siman 25: \"the spirit of God fluttered over them\"; see there and understand; examine well:",
  "114|rabbi-akiva-eiger|5|_": "Seif 5 — they return him. In my humble opinion, on Shabbat night if he forgot to say \"Who causes the wind to blow\" — they do not return him, for if he prayed only the seven-blessing [version] he has fulfilled, as stated in siman 268 in Magen Avraham, siman 15 — even in the seven blessings one does not mention rain. Therefore in what he prayed the order of prayer properly but did not mention rain — this does not detract from [fulfillment via] the seven blessings. Doubtful for practical ruling.",
  "114|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6 — that he began \"You are holy.\" If he remembered after beginning the order of Kedushah, \"Let Your name be sanctified\" — he returns to the beginning, for the order of Kedushah is itself the third blessing, except that there are additional words in it. It is as if he began the third blessing — Gan HaMelekh, siman 152.",
  "117|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "(Seif 4) If one did not ask for rain in the rainy season they make him return, etc. NB: Behold I saw in Ba'er Heitev, who brought in the name of Leket Yosher: in the law, if one continued in the Shabbat prayer and began in \"Atah Chonen\" and finished in Birkas HaShanim, and if he remembered it was Shabbat and it was summer days and he asked for rain — he returns until he says Birkas HaShanim properly and then goes to Shabbat, etc.; thus far. And behold I say: let the matter be silenced and not said, for lechatchilah one should not pray weekday except if one began — then he completes that berachah and no more. If so, it would work out if he had already begun that berachah; but if he had not yet begun, certainly he has no [permission] to begin. If so, in this, since he asked rain in it, it is as if he did not pray that berachah and must begin anew; and if he comes to begin anew, behold today is Shabbat and he has no [permission] to begin weekday. And it appears this law would depend on what the poskim dispute in siman 108: if one prayed on Shabbat and did not remember it is Shabbat, or on Rosh Chodesh and did not remember Yaaleh VeYavo — whether he is required to return and pray weekday or not. There are those who hold: since at night also one does not mention, why should he return and pray? And that is: although he did not pray properly, nevertheless he is not like one who did not pray at all, only considered as one who prayed but not properly, and from that this would not be corrected. But others hold that he must return and pray weekday, from the force that they hold he is like one who did not pray at all, and therefore must return and pray with stipulation. And therefore here too: if one asked for rain in Birkas HaShanim, for those who hold that one who did not act according to law is like one who did not pray — here too it is as if he did not pray Birkas HaShanim at all; and since he did not pray it at all, behold if he remembered at the beginning before he began it, he has no [permission] to begin except in the Shabbat [prayer]. And therefore here too he has no [obligation] to return and say Birkas HaShanim, only begin with Shabbat. But according to those who hold that although not properly, nevertheless he is like one who prayed — if so here too he is as one who began Birkas HaShanim only did not finish; and since he began it, he is required to finish that berachah; therefore he is required to return and say Birkas HaShanim and afterwards begin Shabbat. However for practical halacha it appears all agree here that he is like one who did not pray, for there regarding Shabbat, since by law even on Shabbat one may pray weekday, etc., therefore it is not relevant to say he is like one who did not pray even though he did not remember Shabbat; and likewise Rosh Chodesh without remembering Rosh Chodesh — since in any case in kum ve'aseh he did not spoil his prayer, only in shev ve'al taaseh; therefore he is not considered in omitting the mention like one who did not pray. But here, where he remembered in action in kum ve'aseh something improper, certainly he is like one who did not pray, and it is as if he had not begun it yet, and he is not required to begin anew. And so it seems the essential practical halacha: safek berachos lehakel, and particularly in a matter whose time is not today, only from the force that he began it — and in this there is doubt, he is not required to begin anew. So it seems to my understanding correct and clear. And the law of Leket Yosher is astounding; let the matter be silenced and not said. And the body of the book Leket Yosher is not in my hands to examine its source in this; perhaps God will grant it reaches my hands and I will explain the matter further, with Hashem's help; examine carefully.",
  "124|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Section 4. When the chazzan repeats the prayer, the congregation should be silent and intend the blessings the chazzan blesses, and answer amen. NB: We have the version in chapter 14 of Shabbat (daf 119): R. Eliezer said: Whoever answers amen with all his strength — they open for him the gates of the Garden of Eden, as it says: \"Open the gates, and let a righteous nation enter, keeper of faithfulness\" — do not read \"keeper of faithfulness\" but \"who say amen.\" What is amen? R. Eliezer said: Hashem, faithful King. In Yalkut Yeshayah siman 26 on the verse \"Open the gates\" he brought this statement, and brought the version of one who said: What is amen? You are a faithful King — end quote. To understand what they argue about: it seems they argue about what I saw in one book — it seems to me in Peri Megadim — who was uncertain whether one should answer amen after one praying quietly and one hears the blessing from him; for at the time of Shemoneh Esreh prayer it is as if the Blessed One stands before a person, as explained in the poskim. Therefore the one who holds \"Hashem, faithful King\" holds one does not answer amen except on blessings aloud, not on blessings quietly; and if so, aloud he is not considered as if the Holy One stands before him — therefore he says in hidden language \"Hashem, faithful King.\" But the other view holds that on blessings quietly too one answers amen, and then the Blessed One is before him — then it is appropriate to say in present language \"You are a faithful King.\" Or one may say the distinction is if one hears one blessing outside the bathhouse while he stands in the bathhouse — and similarly if it is permitted to answer amen, since it is explicit in Shulchan Aruch siman 87 that mundane matters may be said in the holy tongue — if so it is permitted to answer amen in the bathhouse, and similarly. But since amen is an acronym of \"Hashem, faithful King,\" and Tosafot wrote there it must be written thus — if so it is a name and forbidden to say in the bathhouse; but if amen is an acronym of \"You are a faithful King,\" it is permitted to say in the bathhouse. But behold it is explicit in poskim that one does not answer amen in a place that interrupts feces or idolatry — all the more if the answering is in the place of his body, where it is forbidden to bless there. If so, this distinction cannot be said except perforce the distinction as I wrote initially — investigate well.",
  "126|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "(Seif 4): If the prayer leader erred when he prayed quietly he never returns, etc. I would say: see Magen Avraham what he wrote if he already completed his prayer, see there. And in my humble opinion the law is with the author of Biur Halacha, for it is evident from the wording of Shulchan Aruch that wrote \"always returns\" — and what is the wording \"always\"? One cannot say his intent is that even though he already stands in another place he returns to the beginning, for that would not need to write \"always,\" for from the wording \"returns\" it already implies thus if he still stands in the place where he erred — what is the wording \"returns\"? Rather perforce the intent is that even though he already prayed further he returns to the beginning; and why write the wording \"always\"? Rather perforce the intent is always, even if he completed his prayer he returns to the beginning — and it is evident like the view of Biur Halacha; and so it is correct and requires good study.",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|22|_": "(Section 22) We strive to have the Gemara be Israel, etc. P.S. Refer to siman 16 in the name of the Rambam, who taught this law, reading Damor to them means that he is not from them, etc. P.S. And here, in my view, it is very surprising from the Shas, page 68 Decamer, where the RH Nectinan a priest calls priests and Israel does not call priests, it is said to them from their own, if so Hazinan, rather, it is interpreted from Damor to them, rather from theirs, and Nihu Damasik There Abei Dalit Halkata Krahah we were only dela in our eyes that it should be exactly theirs but Manel to demand a reversal Deduka is not theirs and the HT for us to renew that they differ in the opposite opinions DLRah will be the interpretation say their own and Abaye will mean the exact reversal of what they say and he is not of them",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|23|_": "(Section 23) While the priests are blessing the people, they shall not look, etc., nor shall they look at them. N.B. A. in the Maga, Sec. It is very puzzling because of his words, the Shas did not break the Shas from the middle and the ZA and the DOK:",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|37|_": "(Section 37) A convert to the Catholic Church shall not raise his hands, etc. P.S. Refer to the MS in Torah Innovations from the year 2017 in Parashat Pinchas, where did the Rambam issue this law according to and DOK:",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|38|_": "(Section 68) He who drank a quart of wine at once shall not raise his hand, etc. P.S. Here I will copy here the Rambam's language in MA from the prohibitions of the altar, Halachh 16 and 16, and the din of one who enters from the altar and to the front in this way, and the din of one leaving from there is the same as if he drank a fourth of wine between the hall and the altar, or tore his clothes there and came out, etc., etc. Let him drink wine between the hall and the altar, and according to what they said in the TK Minyan, even when going out to it, even if he drank outside and entered the hall, he must be merciful, mercifully, at the time of her arrival, she would not be drunk In the hall and at the altar, Nami Icha Akal died. And here the beginning of his words has no understanding at all, and Km Shem 20 and Zal wrote the Rabbid AA if according to him there is no need for him to drink wine between the hall and the altar, etc. It seems that the taste was bitter for him to oblige on the way out, that is, he put wine inside and drank there my blood, he gave wine inside, etc. And his words are very confusing in his interpretation He put wine in front of him with him, and I did not become sick with the aid of the latter. but The Rabbad's intention is this, per the Rambam's opinion, the meaning of the TK, and for those who are in need of it, it is obligatory even for the going out to the LL to interpret the Tosefta Demiri by drinking wine in front of the OL to interpret the Demiri to drink wine outside and in the matter of drinking one drink on entering and one on leaving. And that the Rabbinical Delf according to his words in his interpretation of the OT can drink wine inside only if she drank outside she forgets her and regarding the intention of drinking wine and this is clear in The Rabbad and the Dok. But to reconcile the Rambam's words with the above-mentioned issue, it seems that the Rabbinical Council said, \"From where did you and your sons come from?\" Yes, and look at the BCM who stood by it, and in my opinion he has the meaning of it, O Mmash, you and your sons are with you, meaning like you, and as the Sages demanded in No. 17 Gabi rest with you, like you, yes, the meaning is with you, like you, and we were Aaron. And yet it is proven that it is also required for the exit, and this should not be said to be the intention to require two for the entry and the exit. He entered then drank wine and what is similar to you, and in this case the meaning is only that if he drinks wine inside, he is liable for the exit and a dok, and it is possible to settle the words of the KM Model, he interprets the intent of the Rabad, yes, he just asked him what was the difficulty, because YL DKL understood little, understood, and since it is better to tefi the ukami of the plurality on the woman who drank wine inside and charge him one of the ukami on the woman who drank wine outside and charge him two for that The author of the Hakim Donith of the Rabbid Hoi Kion Demi put wine in the face Ach Hoh Malta Dla Shachiha for this Tfi turns out to be a midrash on if she drank outside And charge him two melamarashes from him if he drank internally, and charge him one because he is discolored from the lata that is common and I am benign and well-documented:",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|40|_": "<b>Seif 40.</b> A priest who married a divorcee shall not lift his hands. NB: see in Magen Avraham what he challenged on Rambam, and see what he wrote in his answer in the omissions to my composition Nidrei Zeruzin on Yoreh De'ah siman 228 — there I settled his words per the method of Magen Avraham in additional explanation — see there in my second composition for the omissions on Nedarim — see there and examine well; and it is in my composition in manuscript page 173. And behold regarding this law of a priest who married a divorcee, see what is written in Sifrei, Sefer HaChayim, to settle Rambam's view from the force that they will take for themselves light-minded people and will not heed prohibition vows — see there; and see what I wrote on this in my composition on Yoreh De'ah, laws of bekhor, siman 314 — proof for this from the Gemara in Bekhorot 36b regarding R' Eliezer who brought before him a firstborn and did not know if he was a priest — see there. Until for the honor of a sage he is concerned about prohibition he acts — from there it is proven that we must be concerned for such a case, that he will take for himself light-minded people who will not heed the honor of a sage and will permit for him; for otherwise there is no explanation for the topic there — perhaps he was not concerned except that the three would not permit him since there is a sage, or he is scheming that the three will not permit him since there is no priest with him; and necessarily the concern is that it is possible he will take for himself three light-minded people and they will permit him; and necessarily that he himself is concerned for the honor of a sage — see there and examine well on this.",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|41|_": "(Seif 41) In the gloss — there are those who say that one who has a daughter who converted to idolatry or committed adultery, etc. NB: see in Magen Avraham what he challenged on this, and see in my composition on the Torah, parashat Balak, year 5599, on the verse \"and the people began to commit harlotry,\" what I rejected of Magen Avraham's proofs, seif katan 62, and I brought that one must distinguish between whether she did so twice or in public versus once and in private — for then she does not profane her father; and in adultery too she profanes. And afterward I brought proof from the Torah there that even many times and in public she does not profane her father, from Yalkut there — see there and examine well. And what he challenged on the gloss of Rama mentioned above from the end of tractate Sukkah — see in my composition on Shulchan Aruch, Yoreh De'ah siman 219, page 120, in passing what I wrote to settle there, with Hashem's help, and examine well. (Seif 41) In the gloss — there are those who say that one who has a daughter who converted to idolatry or committed adultery — they no longer obligate him to sanctify her, for her father is profaned. NB: see in Magen Avraham who explained the words of Mordekhai — his intent is that conversion stam implies adultery; and he wrote afterward that he was negligent in not teaching her this in her childhood, etc. — see there. And his words are astounding: for granted if it is known she committed adultery she profanes her father, for stam she did not guard herself in childhood — for if she had guarded herself in childhood she would not have come to harlotry; therefore it is as the twenty-four who say that even in marriage she is burned. But in conversion, when we do not know she committed adultery, only that we say stam she committed adultery — if so, if that guarding in childhood helps that even after conversion she will not commit harlotry, it is difficult why she profanes her father — perhaps in truth she guarded herself from harlotry in childhood and in truth did not commit adultery from the force that she guarded in childhood from gentiles. And if guarding does not help what she guarded in childhood and in truth she did not commit harlotry — then he was not negligent at all, for even if she guarded it would not help since after apostasy there is no guarding; and again afterward his guarding from the outset does not help. And one cannot say Magen Avraham's intent is that we say from the pleasure of harlotry so that it be common to her — for if he had guarded her in childhood it is obvious she would not desire harlotry and would not convert to their religion; this is not so, for there is no proof from Avodah Zarah — from there it does not imply that from pleasure of harlotry she converts to their religion, only that stam she also committed harlotry, and not that the essence of her conversion is from desire for harlotry. And further, what proof does he bring from a married woman — there one may say we see she committed harlotry and did not convert; on this it depends whether he guarded her in childhood or not. But if she converted first, one may say in this that even if he guarded her in childhood she is accustomed to go out since she already converted — and it requires study; examine well.",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|43|_": "(Section 13) After seven days of mourning he carries his palms and within seven days of mourning he came out of the prison at the same time as the Korin priests. P.S. Look at the Maga that he wrote, and Kash Anan, etc. and it seems that if it happened that the law of consecration does not apply to him, such as he has no intention of going to his grave today, Mm will not lift his hands since he did it with joy, and as the Maga's command, and this will depend on the two reasons for the Shach's opinion, since he did not want Onan to lift his hands, and if it is per the Maja's opinion, he will not lift his hands. And here, in the opinion of the Shachak, it is permissible in the 23 One who died and was a priest and had a brother and the act was in the Yot and the Bahkenach was close to the house in such a way that the priests could not carry their hands there. The neighbor was only a Durbanan and Nihu Dhamga obtained from him that it is from the Torah from all the Pan'i places in the covers of PG and PK Dasukha suggested that it is not per the opinion of the Maga and midi sika we did not issue [and refer to my updates to the Jud C. of the 1990s as in the same] and yet there was a doubt that it was sufficient that per the opinion of the commentators it is permissible to defile without the need for burial and per the opinion of the commentators it is permissible to defile MM Delma the adjacent impurity Hoi only Durbanan per the opinion of the above-mentioned Shachak and the above-mentioned Peni and again in Durbanan Maklinan to trust the sovaris Domther without the need for a burial Despek d'rabbanan to make it easier and Hoys SS Kmash who rule on the blood of eggs and Nihu Dahoi Onan mm if he does not want to be buried today or on Shabbat in places where they hold their hands on Shabbat My dead brother is allowed to lift his hands stated in the nearby Bahkenas and others are forbidden to lift their hands. Who is it for the sake of the Maga? And Dok: And here is the statement above that because of the SS it is permissible to convene the Knesset to carry one's hand, this should not be made difficult, even though the authority says it would be permissible to convene in the house next door, but this is not per the KIL, we do not do a Durbanan doubt for the beginning or a Sika doubt, but we were just talking about the authority, but speaking of a mitzva, it seems that it is permissible to do a Durbanan doubt for the beginning. In the Laws of Sukkah C. 1776, a mitzvah does not belong to the prohibition of Da'in, they cancel it, so it is permissible for a Nami to make a doubt in d'rabbanan, and SS to begin with, and therefore to speak a mitzvah is permissible To gather from the 19th Damran, but per the authority, it is forbidden to doin Ushin Ss and doubt Durbanan for the beginning, and so on. I wrote in my novations to the 19th C. RA in response to the OT and ZAK, and it is correct in my opinion also Y. L. Dahoi Ss in the matter that he has a metirin dela mehani but to speak a mitzva that does not belong to him, he has a metirin dalmar there will be no mitzvah and dok:",
  "128|chokhmat-shlomo|45|_": "(Section 15) In the Haga, it is forbidden to use a priest in Efi' in the 17th, faded as above, in dedication if he does not forgive it. NB refer to the 16th regarding the reason for the permission in this because of the Torah's saying and his dedication to make him a stable soul, and he does not have a stable soul if he is not allowed to use the And here are his words Tamohin from the Toss Damas Suta page M. 1B 45 a mitzvah that they raised there Dafi' before 18 The king is forbidden to stand up because of the power of the amirinan mitzva which I am only regarding the mitzva which is greater than the mitzvos of the king but in standing up before 18 The king's mitzvos are greater than him because the Torah said Let his faith be upon you and not your faith upon him because of the content of their intent. And it is difficult to say that if the king is not allowed to stand in front of the 18th, it is not resting on her soul, and the king's tears do not have his honor, and he does not care for her. To keep a watchful eye on her soul, and how much more the priest can dance. His consecration to be a sanctuary for the priest and there is no mitzvah for the priest to dedicate the 18th and 19th. Oh, the honor of the priest is greater than the 18th and thus the priest may go over his consecration because of the honor of the sage and he has no. His consecration is not said in the place of 18. The king did not say yes, and therefore it seems that the words of the priest must be proved to be false to the priest's forgiveness. And here, per their words, he has MS in Shas Gabi Rav Hona Bar Natan Dahoi Kaim Kedem Shabur Malka and Hoi Medlai' Hameina and said from the Kingdom of Priests and Goy Kadosh Katib Beko. And apparently Tivat and a holy nation it is unnecessary to say from the kingdom of priests as a cry. But his intention is that a king who violates his honor does not have honor from honor, and even a king is not allowed to honor his honor because it is not considered a mitzvah ak ha'anm, what does that mean? Yes, he is a foreigner. For this he reasoned in his language Deshani and that I am the king of Israel is also a holy nation in general and there is no difference between it in 18 only one virtue which is 18 and is called a kingdom of priests therefore in this the commandments of the king are greater than the 18 commandments but since Israel is with her I have made a kingdom of priests and a holy nation it is written in it that you have two virtues and if only one virtue that I am a king again, against him the 18 commandments are first. And no one is allowed to dance for his honor. And the excuse of his words is not to make it difficult for him if he does not want to keep the Torah Peshita If you want to obey the Torah, it is a mitzvah to honor Israel, and if you want to obey the Torah, it is a mitzvah, to honor it. Again, a king who violates his honor does not have honor. A great TAH for this is allowed to forgive his honor Damtzah which I am well informed: and here is the law of the MS in the Haga it is forbidden to use a priest Afi' at this time Faded as raised in the dedication, if it is not condoned for it, refer to the 16th and 16th, what they extended in this, and in my case I did not know what made it difficult for them from the act of Darat and from what Rabbi Abd Cohen had and from what Damarinan Dain Abd Cohen was wounded. After all, there is a difference between 18 and 18, of course it is forbidden to use in a priest a faded 18 raised in the holy, but 18 which is more important than a priest as a priest. Through the law and law of the priesthood, this is proven in the preceding Datah, and also the Sages demanded in No. Nadar Datah, they are called priests per the text, and the cousins were priests, and the interpretation there is to open Rishon, etc. is proven in siman 16 itself is called a priest and siman 16 did not forbid in Jerusalem only for Israel to use a kohen, but a priest in a kohen is allowed to be used and an ak machsh in siman 19 which is allowed to be used in a priest and an ak for the purpose of siman 16. And here is the M.S. Rama Dam Mahal Mahani and likewise the Maga l'fi da'at C. RA brought in the name of the SMG Damhani Mhilah, but the 16th Sal Dla Mahani Mhilah, only a faded power of honor, because it is honor to serve in this way, but in the Alma LM Mhilah and his words are Tamohin Dahari Mukhila In all of Israel, if the priest honors another in the Behmaz, another blesses, and after all, we will learn from his dedication to bless first, and yet he can dance, and why can he be used as an excuse, and both of them will be studied from one scripture, his dedication, and ZA and DOK in this:",
  "128|rabbi-akiva-eiger|20|_": "Seif 20 — it is preferable that he finish. And if he did not intend the prayer of the chazzan — he is confused. Nevertheless he begins 'Place peace.' See in Beit Yosef.",
  "128|rabbi-akiva-eiger|42|_": "Seif 42 — to read from the Torah. See in Mishbetzot Zahav siman 235.",
  "128|rabbi-akiva-eiger|44|_": "Section MD was used in all these countries. In S. Derech HaKedesh Lahagaon Muharram Alfandri D. 20 see this is new what I found in the glosses of M. Bezalel, etc. And I heard that most Ashkenazim do not have the 21st once in the new month and during the periods of the year in the High Court it is possible that they are afraid of this in the prohibition of Akal:",
  "131|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "<b>(Seif 4.)</b> They are accustomed not to fall on their faces, not in the house of a mourner, etc. N.B. Here — regarding a deceased who was not given burial, in the matter that there is upon him no obligation of meals and no aninut — if they pray beside him, whether they say Tachanun or not, this depends on the two reasons why Tachanun is not said in a house of mourning: for the one reason, as it is written \"And I will turn your festivals into mourning\" — if so, in this case where the deceased lies and mourning has not yet taken effect upon him, one may say Tachanun; but for the other reason, that the measure of the Merciful One is extended — if so, all the more so after death before burial, that the measure of the Merciful One is extended with certainty — therefore one should not say Tachanun; so it appears to me in my humble opinion, correctly — examine well.",
  "132|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2 in the gloss: and there are those who wrote to be careful to say pitum haketoret from the text and not by heart etc. Noda B'Yehuda: behold this is only on the understanding that is simple from what is written \"whoever occupies himself with the portion of the olah, it is as if he offered an olah\" — namely as if he offered it himself; so too he said regarding pitum haketoret \"and if one omitted one of its spices he is liable to death.\" But according to what is written in my composition Orach Chayyim siman 1, and I proved that speech is not literally like offering but only as if he handed it to the kohen to offer — and so I found in Tosafos Shabbos — and it is recognized, for behold we are certainly impure from the dead and laymen; therefore he is certainly not liable to death if he hands incense missing one of its spices to the kohen; and perhaps this is Beit Yosef's reason, who brought Magen Avraham who wrote that one is not liable to death through speech; and perhaps it is from this reason, and understand well. (There) in the gloss: and they say orphan's kaddish after Aleinu etc. Noda B'Yehuda: behold a case came before me of one who had a doubt whether his father died on the 20th of the month or the 21st, and in any case on one of the two days he died; and he had a doubt whether his father died on the 19th or the 20th or the 21st; and on the 19th there were other yahrzeits and the one in doubt could not say kaddish; and on the 20th and 21st both doubts came to say kaddish, and this one in doubt claims that he is in doubt only on these two days that he will say the kaddishim on these two days, for then on one day he will certainly say as required, but on the second even on both days it remains doubt; therefore he is preferable. I was asked what his law is, and behold I saw in responsa Me'ir Netivim, who was asked on this and brought proof from Yevamos daf 96 regarding five whose offspring were mixed up, that we do not say one should perform yibum for all of them — it proves that if there is doubt that perhaps each one fulfills the mitzvah, it is preferable to what fulfills only one alone — see there. And in my humble opinion this is not proof: one, that they say there according to what Tosafos wrote in Pesachim regarding five whose Pesach hides were mixed up, who challenged there that behold semichah is not indispensable, and they answered that it is better one enter in doubt of karet than the majority act improperly — see there; it is implied explicitly from their words that there is a difference whether there were only two between whom there is doubt which acted improperly, and whether the majority acted properly — then they may not now act improperly for the minority who has doubt of karet; if so, here too perhaps there specifically because there are five in this manner, for it is impossible to fulfill the mitzvah with certainty except if one performs yibum for all; and if so, if we say to fulfill the mitzvah with certainty, only one would act and he is the minority among all; but if each performs yibum for one, they make each a doubtful mitzvah, and a doubtful mitzvah is also a mitzvah; therefore it is more preferable that the majority do a doubtful mitzvah than the minority do a certain mitzvah; and especially since there it applies per the one who says the mitzvah of yibum takes precedence — if so, if we say they all perform chalitzah, they would certainly act improperly; therefore since the majority would not fulfill the mitzvah of yibum but rather the majority would act improperly and perform chalitzah for their yevamah, therefore the majority may not act improperly for the individual who would act properly. And perhaps even there, if there were only two mixed up, we would say one should perform yibum for all and one would do a certain mitzvah rather than both do doubt; and even if we say that also with two the law is so that each performs yibum for one and chalitzah for one — nevertheless it is not similar to here, for if we say one performs chalitzah for all, then this one certainly acts improperly per the one who says the mitzvah of yibum takes precedence; if so, just as this one would do a certain mitzvah, so the two would certainly act improperly in reverse; and whatever they act improperly is only through this one acting properly; therefore what is the strength of this from that that this one should certainly act improperly that this one should certainly act properly — therefore both should do doubt. But in our case, if the one in doubt does not say kaddish on the 20th and 21st, he is not certainly acting improperly, for perhaps it is not his yahrzeit at all but his time was on the 19th of the month; if so, this one only does doubt of acting improperly, not certainly; and this second one would act certainly properly; and even though he did not say kaddish on the 19th, if so this one too would act certainly improperly — nevertheless what he did not say on the 19th did not cause him but another, and not through this one acting certainly properly did he cause this one to act certainly improperly, but through another; but through him there accrued to him only doubt of acting improperly; therefore here the certain is preferable to the doubt. But there, whatever this one acts improperly is only through this one acting properly; therefore what is the strength of this from that? And further, there it was active to say to this one to act and perform chalitzah in order to perform yibum for one — there the other can say \"what is your certainty to me that I should act improperly so that you act properly, and my doubt is preferable to your certainty\" — a person is close to himself. But here we do not force the second to act, but it is sit and do not act, and we are in doubt about the congregation praying there to whom they will give more kaddish, to this one or that one; therefore regarding others to whom both are equal one like the other, we say it is more fitting to give kaddish to this one who will say certainly than to this one who will say only in doubt; and in my humble opinion it is correct, not like the opinion of Sefer responsa Me'ir Netivim mentioned above, and understand well, and so I found.",
  "135|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Section B. If they canceled one Shabbat, reading the parasha in public, I was asked in one city who did not read Parsha Pinchas in public on Shabbat, what would they do, and I replied that there is a rule for that, because on the second Shabbat, there will be matots and masei connected, and there is no rule for halchain, etc. 3 Parasyot What is this or that to me? And if we come to it, we will definitely take what was at the beginning of 2 Parasyots, he has it, yes, dam, later on. And although it's benign, there's no regulation for it, and it's simple:",
  "137|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Seif 3 — even if he skipped one verse, and even one word — Gan HaMelekh, Ba'al HaGvulim siman 10.",
  "139|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3 in hagahah — one whose father converted to idolatry: they call him by his paternal grandfather's name, but not his name alone, so as not to embarrass him publicly. NB — see Taz Moed Katan 1; see Avraham Zuckerman who rejected his words that in a get the rabbi must investigate the father's name — his answer did not help: (1) the rabbi need investigate only if he is not established in the city by his name and father's name; but if established in the city by his name and father's name, no need to investigate further — so too here, if he is established to go up to Torah as Abraham, witnesses testify he is established to be called by his father's name thus, and no need to investigate more; (2) is a get required specifically a rabbi? If he divorced alone before witnesses and wrote the get himself in his handwriting and witnesses signed and gave it to her — is she not divorced b'dieved? If so, concern he could write a get himself, sign witnesses, divorce her, write the name Abraham, and she goes and marries another — and the Taz wrote well; see Chidushei HaEzer siman 129 on these words of the Taz, and examine.",
  "139|rabbi-akiva-eiger|11|_": "{Rama: gloss seif 11} And from this they practiced. And so it is in Tractate Soferim chapter 17 halacha 8.",
  "141|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4 — through an agent. And even if in any case one stands to show the place of the reading — see Levush and in Gan HaMelekh siman 51.",
  "143|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4, in the gloss — one must compromise; meaning, this is the way of resolution. But the view of Agur is that even in a complete error one does not take out another; and the view of Beit Yosef is that even for missing and extra letters one takes out another — but Rama personally decided thus as halakha; and so is explained in Darkei Moshe, Yoreh Deah siman 279; and see in responsum Minchat Yaakov siman 18.",
  "147|chokhmat-shlomo|8|_": "Section 8 of the Maga sak 11 MS and a deviation, page 35 Amarinan, etc. P.S. Here, being in this issue, it is difficult for me to be a good person there, what is the difference there, and they do not do mitzvos of bundles of bundles of religion. The Delrash interprets the reason that there should not be a rude heart in her company, so it is true that it belongs to this reason, but in the Alma, it does not belong to the reason why we do matzos of bundles of bundles and R. Kamer is not from the name but because he dictated it alone and not with another with her. And Doc:",
  "150|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4. NB: see in Magen Avraham seif katan 4 what he wrote — namely only when it was a partnership, etc.; but see in my composition on Choshen Mishpat siman 194, in Shulchan Aruch Choshen Mishpat siman 254, what is astounding on this and from where is this derived — see there and examine well.",
  "150|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "Seif 4 — One who builds opposite a window in the synagogue — in teshuva Teshurat Chayim to Maharachash part 1 siman 11 he elaborated on this and concluded it is a lone opinion. And see in teshuva Mahar Elazar Sasson siman 192 and in teshuva Noda B'Yehuda part 2 siman 16 who wrote one should not reject Agudah's words, but in any case widening eight cubits suffices per Keneset HaGedolah Choshen Mishpat siman 154 in Tur letter 71.",
  "151|chokhmat-shlomo|11|_": "Seif 11 — But in its settlement a condition does not help, etc. See what Magen Avraham questioned on this; and see what he wrote in his settlement in his novellae on Yoma, as the case of all the offices that were in the Temple — they did not have a mezuzah, on daf 14; see in my work on Orach Chayim, year 1799, what he wrote on this in its settlement, and Pilpul on the view of Rambam there — see there and study.",
  "151|rabbi-akiva-eiger|3|_": "Section C, but in the Bahmad it is allowed. The source of the law was cited in the BJ in the name of Mahari Habib. And C. Afi' Shinat Arai is forbidden in the Bahkan, but in the Bahmad ap Dahmir of the Bahkan appears to be a domtar. Damarinan P. Who died, Tzipi Debi Rav Dehani Garsi and Hani Gani Akal. He felt that the difficulty of the law should be made easier in the High Court than in the Synagogue. But it was proved by the blood of his dead, and this evidence is very puzzling. Dahari Amri' PD Damgala page 28 said Raba 18 and their students are allowed. And they made it difficult there from Ovada Darb Ada and Rabina Shem Damri Lao for no reason and it was said in this 3 reasons the opinion of the Ashiri glosses they made it harder on themselves and the opinion of the Rambam Derek Medochah Sheri. Rabbi Derek's opinion in the Bahmad is that it is permissible to eat food, because everyone has excuses for eating food, they are allowed to enjoy and eat in the Bahmad, it is pushed aside, as in siman 19, it is like eating a meal, and the Rambam and the Ghat Ashiri of the Tah are also allowed in the Bahkenas. And the Ran only in the Bahmad, even though it is not pushed. In the Bihamad more than in the Biahkenas Daha 18 are truly allowed to enjoy in the Bihamad and the 33rd.",
  "153|chokhmat-shlomo|14|_": "(Seif 14) NB: see in Magen Avraham seif katan 36 what he challenged from here to Yoreh De'ah; and see in my composition on Yoreh De'ah, fourth edition, in the responsum to the community of Kazlav, what I wrote to settle this correctly, with Hashem's help, and several distinctions in law that emerge from it; and what the Mechaber wrote — Reuven who said 'this land I give,' etc. — see what is relevant to this in my composition on Choshen Mishpat, fourth edition, siman 221, in the responsum on the laws of damage from neighbors, siman 103, for the community of Tarnopol — see there.",
  "153|chokhmat-shlomo|20|a": "Seif 10 — and there are those who forbid except to learn Torah or to marry a woman. NB: see in Yoreh De'ah siman 270 what I settled there regarding what Magen Avraham challenged here from siman 248 and on Beit Yosef in the law of other books — see there — that I clarified support for this from the explanation of Rashbam that even tefillin are forbidden to sell unless to learn Torah or to marry a woman — see there and examine well.",
  "153|chokhmat-shlomo|20|b": "(Seif 20) There are those who say that a Torah scroll that was established as having belonged to Reuven's ancestors — the congregation cannot retain it. See in my composition on Avodah Zarah, Yoreh De'ah edition, siman 4, that it found its place; in Avodah Zarah siman 125 there I wrote, with Hashem's help, in passing, regarding the community of Senatin: regarding one who had a Torah scroll in this synagogue and went to another synagogue and took the Torah scroll with him and died — whether the son-in-law of his daughter may take the Torah scroll to return it to the first synagogue where he had prayed initially — see there, in passing, with Hashem's help, and examine well.",
  "153|chokhmat-shlomo|21|_": "(Seif 21) In the gloss — it is forbidden to make from the payment of a harlot or the price of a dog a matter of mitzvah. NB: behold, if one had relations with those liable to court-imposed death penalties — whether the payment is permitted; and see in my composition on Bava Kamma in the chapter 'Marveh' in the topic of 'Akutz Te'enah,' etc., what I wrote to prove from the language of Rambam that for relations with court-imposed death the payment is permitted — see there; its reason is correct, with Hashem's help, and examine well.",
  "154|chokhmat-shlomo|11|_": "In Heaven’s Word, Heaven’s promise is not to do so. Heaven’s Word is in the Gemara if it is possible to receive from Heaven’s Word I pray that I pray to Heaven’s people, and I pray to Heaven’s people, and I pray to them",
  "154|chokhmat-shlomo|8|_": "In the Rama gloss they practice enjoying many kinds of benefit from holy objects — not addressed; see Taz at note 7 what he wrote \"were it not that I fear,\" etc.; see my chiddushim on Yoma 12b, as the gemara states there that \"two\" is not fit neither for the High Priest nor for an ordinary priest, because of \"elevate in holiness and do not lower\" — there I proved against his view; and especially what he said there (4) that he would have trouble in life, and it would be an inference that he would never be a priest — yet nevertheless \"elevate in holiness and do not lower\" applies to him; understand well. And what Taz wrote in the name of Maharshal that it is not called lowering as long as the sefer is inside it — and so wrote Magen Avraham in his name; and Bach brought that even this is lowering from holiness; understand well. And see my chiddushim on the Laws of Chanukah siman 671 what I wrote on this — proof for Bach that even this is lowering from holiness; understand well what I wrote on this and examine. And it appears to my humble opinion to bring proof to Maharshal's view that Taz brought above — that lowering from holiness for a time does not apply — from what we learn in chapter 4 of Sukkah 44b: since you say it is only taken by itself, obviously he is obligated in the willow in the lulav. And it is difficult: why does he ask — let him say it teaches us that even if he took it from the lulav he does not fulfill with it? And they answered: the reason is, in truth he does not fulfill this way if it is by itself; but one can say: since we hold it is forbidden to lower from grave holiness to light holiness, it is proven that this is contempt of a mitzvah; and therefore mitzvah objects may be discarded — that is, after their mitzvah time has passed; but during their mitzvah time they are forbidden for benefit and to use them from the power of contempt of a mitzvah, as written in Ne'ot Sukah and the like. And if so, even to use them for a light mitzvah is also included in contempt of a mitzvah. And if so, one can say: since the lulav is from the Torah in the borders one day, and the willow has no fundamental source from the Torah in the borders, and they make the lulav all seven as a remembrance of the Temple, and the willow one day — and if so it was a light mitzvah, and therefore one should not remove it from grave holiness to light holiness. And necessarily, with the lulav — even though this is not contempt, as is proven here, that while the books are still inside it is permitted to place in it also other books — either he cannot fulfill the willow, since it is only taken by itself; or if he took it from the lulav, again it is lowering from holiness for a time, and it is forbidden to remove it from grave to light, since it is still fit for the grave today. And one cannot say that in any event b'dieved why does he not fulfill — and behold the Tanna said he does not fulfill his obligation with the willow in the lulav, which implies even b'dieved; let one say it is a mitzvah that comes through transgression — and it is difficult for most poskim who hold Pasul even b'dieved. And what does he ask — perhaps while it is still fit for the mitzvah, as it is forbidden for mundane use, so too it is forbidden to change it to light holiness, which is also included in contempt of a mitzvah; and necessarily lowering for a time is not lowering; and since it is still possible to return it to the lulav, it is not considered lowering; and even in holiness it is permitted in such a case — how much more so with mitzvah objects. And it is proven like Maharshal's view, not like Bach; examine well.",
  "158|rabbi-akiva-eiger|11|_": "{Rama: gloss seif 11} He blesses afterward — if he blessed 'on all that exists' after Birkat HaMotzi before eating, it appears it is not an interruption — see below siman 167 seif 7 and in Magen Avraham.",
  "158|rabbi-akiva-eiger|4|_": "{Rama: gloss seif 4} Wine, oil — olive oil — so in Beit Yosef. And see responsum Gevurat Anashim part 1 siman 29.",
  "159|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Section 7, but if he gives them, this is from the words of the rabbi. It was brought up in B. B. and in the N.D. It is puzzling about the author according to Damboar in section 8. It seems to the author that Dznur is a vessel, and it is not mehni when he dips his hands in the vessel. There is a baptism on it and also because of taking it is invalid since it is connected as if it is not a case of taking, and the RA ruled that 11 Dazil per the rule of the Tos and the Rash that you want on the BHAG a deznor is not a vessel and this is the reason itself is difficult Sha'a Dahoi a vessel in the Holy Land is not allowed to be immersed in a vessel. Even if it is kosher, that is, it is not permissible to dip one's hands into a vessel, but a vessel that is connected to the ground is not a vessel. And between that, from the law of a kosher vessel and from the law of a kosher land, there is a need for a mixed taste. Taking Lika Kion Delika kach Gabra and Tzag:",
  "159|rabbi-akiva-eiger|9|_": "Section 9 is tilted in the land and the water is showering in the land of the people. And the Demota ruling is kosher and so on, the author here is just saying that he did not take it, and in the Sah the ruling of Dvasat HaDhak is to be trusted by Abhag and Tsa:",
  "168|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "Seif 6—pat haba'ah b'kisnin one blesses borei minei mezonot etc. Footnote: I saw in Peri Megadim he was in doubt in law of pat haba'ah b'kisnin if one ate satiety measure whether liable to bless from Torah or only rabbinically. Ostensibly one can resolve this doubt from Talmud first chapter of Arakhin daf 4 where it says all are liable in zimmun—priests, Levites, Israelites—and challenges obvious, it is needed when they ate kodashim, lest one say Merciful One said and they shall eat those through whom atonement was made and behold atonement was made—teaches us and you shall eat and be satisfied Merciful One said and behold they are there—end Talmud; and it is hard: bread we do not find in sacrifice except in menachot—for in meat birkat hamazon does not apply and necessarily you find this novelty only in menachot—and in menachot there is oil and one does not fulfill with matzah and automatically no obligation of birkat hamazon at all—how does it say they are liable in zimmun; necessarily it refers to eating satiety measure through meal satiety and on this it says and you shall eat and be satisfied Merciful One said and behold they are there—if so it implies birkat hamazon applies from Torah. And do not say it refers to challot of thanksgiving that one fulfills with matzah and is not rich matzah and automatically liable in birkat hamazon—for in thanksgiving it is not relevant to say and they shall eat those through whom atonement was made for it is not for atonement. But per this our proof is not—for then you do not find it in minchah that it is for atonement except minchat choteh which is for atonement—and in minchat choteh there is no oil and it is not pat haba'ah b'kisnin; nevertheless one can prove reverse that all birkat hamazon on pat haba'ah b'kisnin is only rabbinic—for otherwise it is hard who compelled Talmud to explain reason that it was thought they are exempt from power of behold atonement was made—for then novelty would be only in minchat choteh alone; we also need to invent new reasoning that what is atonement does not have berachah—which is against reasoning [though true one can say from this proof for view of those who hold where one eats under compulsion he does not bless—and therefore it was thought since kohanim are compelled to eat it and Merciful One compelled them and he did not wish to eat only Torah forced him to eat—therefore it was thought berachah does not apply—therefore teaches since written and you shall eat and be satisfied and behold they are there; and per Talmud's conclusion one can say reverse even one who eats under compulsion blesses since written and you shall eat and be satisfied and they are there—and so on at length]. In any case it is hard why Talmud needed this extra—it should have answered it teaches in other menachot even though they did not eat in way of measure others establish on it—and if so it was thought they are exempt since it is pat haba'ah b'kisnin—therefore teaches since mitzvah in eating them mitzvah establishes him as kavua—therefore teaches kohanim are liable in zimmun even less than establishment measure since mitzvah in eating them mitzvah makes kavua as written in Talmud chapter 5 of Beitzah eating of Shabbat establishes for tithe and so many places; and proven from there eating of matzah is not considered casual only kavua and necessarily mitzvah established it—and if so teaches here too with kohanim mitzvah makes kavua and even less than measure others establish they are liable in zimmun—and therefore proven even real establishment is only rabbinic—and here itself is rabbinic enactment—cannot say regarding kohanim they enacted to bless also on less from power mitzvah considered it kavua for that is enactment on enactment which we do not do—and if so we do not say mitzvah makes kavua except in matter that is from Torah or at least establishment itself is from Torah—therefore it helps also to say mitzvah established it; but for birkat hamazon whose establishment measure itself is only rabbinic and from Torah does not depend on establishment only satiety—and what they enacted for pat haba'ah b'kisnin the measure as way others establish is only rabbinic—cannot say mitzvah established it for enactment on enactment; and there is proof from this it is only rabbinic; and I had place to extend but time did not allow—perhaps Heaven will grant another time to explain more—examine well.",
  "170|rabbi-akiva-eiger|19|_": "Seif 19 — and to give to his son, and all the more to give to another — forbidden; responsum Maharit part 1 siman 130.",
  "171|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Section B did not sit on a full line of cells. See stated in the box that it is circumcised, etc., and see it in there A year B. Yossi says if he has given him a full piece of clothes while she is plucking a hundred shelves if she is not slumbered by a shelf and says that she is a wild sight In the words of there and DCO:",
  "178|chokhmat-shlomo|6|_": "Seif 6: One who remembered during the meal he did not pray. In margin: if he had no time at all to wait even a moment or two, see Taz siman 471 s.k. 4 that from his words it appears in such case it is interruption and must bless; but per Magen Avraham there s.k. 16 it is proven even in such case no blessing needed—requires study.",
  "178|chokhmat-shlomo|7|_": "(Seif 7) A person who sleeps during his meal—casual sleep is not interruption. In margin: see Magen Avraham who wrote in any case we require netilat yadayim for it is hesech da'at, as Tur and he brought proof from oral law, etc. In my humble opinion this is not clear—room to distinguish per Tosafot Chagigah 21a s.v. the mourner and lacking atonement who wrote: R' Yitzchak answered hesech da'at to disqualify only similar to there when it was in his hand and he diverted mind from it; but when not in his hand such as lost—not applicable to disqualify; R' Eliezer brought proof from ch. 3 that limbs of olot and pieces of chatat were found in azarah—implies olah he sacrifices and chatat is eaten and not disqualified by hesech da'at, end quote. [Long discussion of Shekalim/Yerushalmi, R' Yitzchak vs R' Elazar on lost items and hesech da'at in azarah—concludes casual sleep during meal is not hesech da'at like loss, unlike speaking during meal or Hallel/aggadah; if he put himself to sleep knowingly it is hesech da'at; if sleep overcame him without lying down intentionally—not hesech da'at; for netilat yadayim rabbinic law follows lenient view when doubt. Previous seif prayer: if no time even moment see Taz 471:4 interruption must bless; Magen Avraham 471:16 no blessing—even in margin of Shulchan Aruch.]",
  "179|chokhmat-shlomo|5|_": "Seif 5 — those who read in the house of the homeowner to eat kinds of fruits. NB: see in Taz who argues on this law, and in Avodat Rachel he rules like Shulchan Aruch; and in my humble opinion it seems the law is with Taz, for even if the intent of Tosafot is as Beit Yosef understood and as Peri Megadim wrote that so is our reading in the Gemara — their words themselves are astounding: for certainly what relevance does the homeowner's reliance have there? Within the meal we do not require his intent certainly, and even if he did not have intent it is permitted to eat within the meal; only where he explicitly removed himself from eating it is forbidden to eat more. Therefore automatically the homeowner's reliance is a doubt and he does not make removal, and it remains stam as if they did not say 'give us and we will bless.' But regarding fruits — if those from the first courses are not before him, then by law he must bless on them; only if one says he had explicit intent he need not bless on them. And from where is it derived that the homeowner's reliance creates intent to be as if he certainly intended? Perhaps he does only a doubt or stam. Therefore the essential ruling is like Taz. And what Avodat Rachel wrote — safek berachot lehakel — this is astounding, for that applies where it is not a berachah of enjoyment, or even in a berachah of enjoyment if he already ate — for Birkat HaMazon he cannot exempt in another matter. But before the berachah, neither to exempt nor to bless if he has a doubt — examine well.",
  "183|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4 and send to his wife she drink—Mishna Berurah wrote even if did not eat with them as Gemara daughter-in-law; wonder why proof from amoraic woman not explicit braita Bava Metzia 86 Sarah—angels knew Sarah in tent why asked to endear to husband R' Yosi: send cup of blessing—proves though Sarah did not eat sent cup—wonder on Magen Avraham; cannot check if commentators noticed; writing in village at hot bath; but precise Magen Avraham right—initially difficult R' Yosi send cup of blessing as answer why asked—finally hard since knew place send without asking where is Sarah; seems question falls on two matters: if asks generally where is person only if don't know place; if many guests one belongs to meal as guest sees wife not at meal may ask where is so-and-so metaphorically why not come to bread—even if know place question why not come to bread; place settle tannaitic difficulty knew in tent why asked—perhaps intent why not dine with us falls even knowing place; but Gemara does not answer so difficulty why not dine—must be intent where is Sarah; since knew why asked endear; R' Yosi: intent of question where is Sarah why not dine—not modesty—for without dining no cup of blessing; if dines earns cup—should not be stringent prevent blessing; not permitted such stringency; therefore send cup of blessing; question why not dine; Magen Avraham did not bring braita proof could imply opposite—brought daughter-in-law obligated send though not eating; must explain sugya differently: dispute if woman not dining better she come drink cup or they send—reward of steps; angels and Sarah disputed reward of steps like disputes upper and lower academy—angels held reward hers should come; asked Sarah your wife knew in tent intent metaphorically why not come drink cup; R' Yosi: question in language where is Sarah why not come drink; if question why not come truly to drink—intent send cup preferable not she come; requires study; seems correct; see Chiddushei HaRav on Eruvin siman 21 Biur on s.v. to endear see there.",
  "184|chokhmat-shlomo|4|_": "Seif 4 but does not know if blessed—see Magen Avraham s.7 wrote fourth blessing too lest treat lightly; clear proof Pesachim 39b Rambach asked fulfill marror with maaser sheni etc.; matzah Torah not fulfill marror rabbinic or no difference Rava reasonable matzah marror he lacks understanding Rashi explained equated—equating only Torah not rabbinic; per above intent if not matzah marror separately say matzah Torah not fulfill marror rabbinic; since together matzah not fulfill if say marror rabbinic fulfills comes treat rabbinic lightly—Rava reasonable since together treat rabbinic lightly marror not fulfill—correct in Gaza. After writing saw Penei Yehoshua sensed Rashi difficulty how equating rabbinic—explains since matzah equated marror marror equated matzah marror Torah not fulfill maaser sheni same rabbinic all enact like Torah—wondrous why Rava novelty Rambach explicit or Torah not fulfill; only since rabbinic; can say Rashi intent Gitin 65 other said enact like Torah only what has Torah root without root not enact like Torah; challenges maaser now rabbinic proof no root; or even without root enact like Torah—Rava reasonable matzah marror equal matters related considered marror has Torah root when has root enact like Torah correct in Gaza. Law Shulchan Aruch siman 184 seif 4 but does not know must return bless from doubt Torah—see Magen Avraham wrote fourth blessing lest treat lightly; difficulty siman 67 doubt shema returns reads blesses Taz reason doubt Torah also bless; Magen Avraham Rashba Rambam enactment etc.; why not reason treat rabbinic lightly since doubt both if read shema without blessings treat rabbinic lightly—must say specifically fourth blessing since otherwise lenient workers therefore enacted not answer amen aloud after lest treat lightly therefore concern if see not return from doubt treat lightly as Magen Avraham Bach amen quietly; now no worker distinction may answer aloud—here if doubt blessed no fourth blessing no lightness now; R' Shimon Chayon Magen Avraham deal with Gemara law; or R' Shimon Chayon amen too since Gemara concern lightness applies to us. If Bach opposite no lightness concern—again doubt question should not bless doubt; seems so in my view; in Sefer HaChaim wrote other matter see there; now seems as wrote here correct—there challenged from Tosafot return truly fixed not shema—answer what wrote there fits; here from poskim Rashba words not settled in our answers there—must be as wrote only there concern treat rabbinic lightly not general—clear study well.",
  "188|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6: and he did not mention Yom Tov. And on Rosh Hashanah Yom Tov see Sha'agat Aryeh simanim 101–102. And a woman who forgot to mention Yom Tov in birkat hamazon need not return and bless as explained in my responsum siman 1.",
  "188|rabbi-akiva-eiger|7|_": "Seif 7: its law is like Rosh Chodesh. See Levushei Serad that also in this Chol HaMoed is like Rosh Chodesh—that one must mention in a berachah by itself before he began haTov vehaMetiv even though the poskim did not mention it so—see there. And likewise Eshel Avraham there in Shakhnehag writes only that one should not say Baruch shenatan Yom Tov for Chol HaMoed is not called Yom Tov; rather he should say Baruch shenatan yamim to Israel for joy and gladness.",
  "188|rabbi-akiva-eiger|8|_": "Seif 8: its law is like Rosh Chodesh. And if he ate the second meal close to nightfall and forgot to mention Shabbat and did not remember until after he made havdalah on Motza'ei Shabbat—it appears he does not return and bless since he already made havdalah—see above siman 118 seif 11. And even if he remembered before he made havdalah—the opinion of Ba'al Kisei DeHarsana siman 87 is that he does not return and bless.",
  "190|chokhmat-shlomo|3|_": "Seif 3: the measure of drinking wine to become obligated in a final berachah. See: behold in law there is a dispute among the poskim whether the measure of wine is a kezayit or a revi'it; it appears in my humble opinion there is clear proof that a kezayit suffices—from an explicit mishnah in tractate Me'ilah daf 15b: five things in an olah combine with each other, and six in a todah combine with each other—the flesh, bread, wine, etc.; and it is difficult: how does wine combine with flesh and bread—behold their measures are not equal, for this is a kezayit and that a revi'it; and it is explicit in the mishnah there later that whatever their measures are not equal they do not combine. And I saw in Tosafot Zevachim daf 109, brought in Tosefot Yom Tov on Me'ilah there, that they sensed this and answered: in bread that soaked in wine, which is a way of eating and is a kezayit—see there. But in my opinion their words are astounding—for behold the Talmud concludes in chapter 3 of Pesachim that in kodashim certainly heter combines with issur; and if so, why mention specifically in the mishnah five things in an olah and six in a todah—behold in all korbanot even wine of non-sacred or bread and flesh of non-sacred also combine for a kezayit for piggul, notar, and tamei, for in kodashim heter combines with issur. But behold the Talmud challenges similarly in chapter 3 of Pesachim regarding a nazir, as taught: from all that is made from the vine, wine for nazirite prohibitions combine with each other—and behold heter combines with issur; issur with issur is obvious; and it answers: heter combines with issur at once; issur with issur even one after the other; and if so, here too in what the mishnah teaches—five things in an olah, six in a todah—it deals with one after the other, and therefore heter combining with issur does not apply here; and if so, perforce it deals with one after the other; and if at once, in bread that soaked in wine the conclusion would be that even heter combines with issur; and perforce it deals with one after the other; and if so Tosafot's difficulty returns to its place—for behold their measures are not equal, and why do they combine? Perforce also wine's measure is a kezayit—and this is a great proof in my humble opinion; and understand well.",
  "193|rabbi-akiva-eiger|6|_": "Seif 6 — zimun departed from them; and with this specifically if they did not eat afterward together; but if they ate afterward together they combine.",
  "194|chokhmat-shlomo|2|_": "Seif 2 — three who ate and one of them went out to the market, they call him, etc. It appears to me from this that if he does not join and answer with them he cannot invite zimun, even though at first they ate together and became obligated in zimun — he cannot invite zimun without them; and this is not similar to prayer, for we hold that if one began with Avot among ten he finishes, and many similar cases there that if one began with ten he finishes; and here what he began to eat with three does not help — there must be three at the berachah of zimun itself. It is difficult for me from the sugya in Sanhedrin chapter 1 daf 8, where the tanna taught: zimun with three — what is zimun? If we say the berachah of zimun — but it was taught: zimun and berachat zimun with three; and if you say he is explaining — what is zimun? The berachah of zimun — but it was taught: zimun with three and berachat zimun with three, etc.; and it is difficult why he challenges, and why should we push more that the intention is regarding zimun for judgment where what he paired with berachat zimun is not relevant; rather he should have explained as we thought regarding zimun in birkat hamazon, and what he doubled is when if he had written only one of them one would have thought he teaches only regarding the essence of the law that zimun requires three — to exclude if at first there were not three, to exclude from what we thought that zimun does not require three at all; therefore he teaches that from the outset of the law zimun requires three. Nevertheless it is possible that even if at first they ate with three it suffices, even though at the time of blessing there are not three they can invite zimun; therefore he teaches in the doubling that zimun requires three and it is not enough that there were three at first, only berachat zimun with three — at the time of blessing itself we require three; and why he challenges requires further study. From this it is further difficult for me on what Magen Avraham wrote in siman 191 explaining Tur's words, that behold the Sages were strict in zimun on a fourth berachah — meaning on the body of birkat hamazon that one discharges his fellow, which applies only in zimun with the Name; if so it is difficult why the Gemara challenges — let us say zimun refers to the body of birkat hamazon to discharge one's fellow, and berachat zimun refers to the berachah n'varech she'achalnu, etc.; and if he had written only one, one could say only n'varech requires three but the body of birkat hamazon to discharge one's fellow does not require three; therefore he teaches both to inform us that zimun means discharging one for his fellow in the body of birkat hamazon, and this came and revealed that; and the intention of zimun would be like there in Tur — what he wrote regarding zimun on a fourth berachah; and why he challenges requires further study — examine well.",
};
